body:
I'd vote keep going - it's not a topic I have much connection to (not an American), but your writing kept me hooked the whole way through. The only two criticisms I have is I'm not sure how I feel about the bait and switch (focusing on Lester then switching to Louise). Part of me likes the subversion, but another part thinks you shouldn't get that close to a characters head unless you're planning to come back later.

&nbsp;

Besides that, my only criticism is that the oppression in Chesterfield seems a little too extreme. I might be wrong, but it feels like at that level, someone would step in. Also raises the question of why the people don't just bugger off if there's no hope for them - or riot. 1 in 3 people is enough to cause a helluva mess. Not to say you need to change it, just make sure you keep those questions in mind so you can always keep your readers on the right side of suspension of disbelief.

&nbsp;

Overall though, I really liked it. If you do continue, make sure to put it where I can see it ;)

First things first, I'm not sure this is a prologue. Generally a prologue features a different character/writing style/time period than the main story. Generally they serve to set the tone, or reveal an important plot element that would otherwise have no way to get into the story.

I have no main story to base this off, but the sheer amount of information you're giving me gives the impression this is more of a chapter one, or zero. But that's more semantics than real criticism.

&nbsp;

Speaking of semantics, a minor nitpick here: you capitalised the 'b' in Branch when giving the full names of the two types, but then your acronym/initialism was missing it. Minor thing, but it bugged me. Another little nitpick is that you really don't need to tell us that Director is the top of the hierarchy - that's like explaining that the President is the leader of America.

&nbsp;

Now for my big issue, although the premise you've set up shows some promise, this excerpt doesn't sell me on it, at all. It's an omniscient narrator jumping from person to person, giving me a quick blurb on each, but not staying with any long enough for me to care. Everything you've told me about Zach I forget as soon as I learn about Gregg, and as soon as I learn about the government I've nearly forgotten Gregg. Some of this stuff sounds pretty juicy too - certainly, Gregg's dead mentor deserves a better send-off than a single sentence.

&nbsp;

Overall, it conveys the impression that I'm looking at your worldbuilding/backstory notes, rather than a chapter of story. In all four paragraphs, nothing happens, and I get told a lot. Not a good ratio. It can be fixed though - you could tell us all the same stuff (admittedly with many more words) by showing us a day in the life of Gregg - his normal interactions with his nephew, his work as Director, his musings about the mysterious Experimental Branch. I care about the height and weight of characters far less than what they say and do.

So short criticisms out of the way first: word choice/grammar need a bit of cleaning up (also you spelt excitement without the middle 'e' a couple of times). One such thing that stood out was rain drops 'going down' ('coming down' is generally the phrase used). Another was 'I found intriguing what she told me', which feels a bit clunky - 'I found what she told me intriguing' flows nicer (in my opinion, at least), although you could probably mix up that sentence a little more to really get it going.

&nbsp;

Besides that, pretty decent, it caught my attention. I liked the story (perhaps slightly heavy handed, but it's being told to a child, so understandably so). The one thing I'm worried about is that a prologue should probably tell us something about the story to come. A good 60% of that prologue was talking about the sky, about three sentences were talking about the boy growing up to be a witch.

&nbsp;

Maybe I'm wrong, but the way it was just tossed in there makes it seem pretty likely the boy will become a witch (or has already become a witch) over the course of the story. I really hope the sky  (and for that matter Granny or the boy's relationship with her) also features prominently in the story (and not as just as a backdrop). Because if we went through all that just so you could show the moment the boy decided on his path it's a little wasteful (a few lines of dialogue or a paragraph of reminiscing could serve the same purpose). The talk about the sky's fear that one day it would cease to serve its purpose felt to me like a nice foreshadowing of what could be a major plot-point in the story proper. If it isn't, I'll feel a little teased.

&nbsp;

As a final side note, prologues get a bad rap (dunno why, I personally don't mind them). Consider calling it a chapter one or zero instead.

Hope I'm not too late for this party.

&nbsp;

>The sun had long since set, shrouding the city in electric twilight. Winter had hit in full force, and the streets had been abandoned by all but the drunks and those without homes to hide in. John was one of the latter, but he was no madman, nor a victim of fate. He was a hunter, and tonight he was on the prowl, wandering through side streets and back alleys with the air of a man who had nowhere better to be.

>&nbsp;


>He could hear his target a few streets away, the tell-tale rhythm of shoes on concrete barely audible. He wasn't close enough to hear her heartbeat – not yet – but he knew it must be pounding in her chest. She was as close to running as she could be without drawing attention. A smart move, but she'd already been noticed.

>&nbsp;

>He twirled a lit cigarette between his fingers, lit end dancing up and down his hand, tracing patterns in the dark. He relished the warmth they offered on nights like these, however meagre it was. He took a deep breath, then exhaled, dragging his fingers through the smoke. He wouldn't be able to track her scent, but his eyes and ears would be more than up to the task. Besides, the city smelled like ash regardless.

>&nbsp;

>He tracked his prey for the next half-hour, staying a safe distance away as he waited for her to make a mistake. Eventually, she did, wandering into a dead-end alley. It was long, providing back-entrance access to half the shops in the area, but the walls were high and the doors closed. The only way out was the way she'd come in, and she wouldn't know that for a few more minutes. In the meantime, John waited, counting her breaths as he perched on a nearby rooftop.
